# Debian-provided pieces (the Sysible packages are included via config/packages.chroot)
gnome-core
gdm3
# --- Non-free firmware for real laptop/desktop hardware (bare-metal, not just VMs).
# The build enables the non-free-firmware archive area, but firmware is NOT pulled
# in unless named. Without it, Wi-Fi/NICs are dead on physical machines — e.g. a
# MacBook won't get network in the installer or after install (the first-boot
# vendor/Obsidian fetches then fail, and boot can stall waiting for a network that
# never comes up). firmware-linux pulls the broad set; the rest cover the common
# wireless chips.
firmware-linux
firmware-linux-nonfree
firmware-misc-nonfree
firmware-brcm80211
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-realtek
firmware-atheros
# Broaden Wi-Fi coverage to the rest of the common laptop/desktop chips (all are
# Architecture:all firmware blobs, so they're safe on every arch and just sit
# unused when the matching hardware isn't present):
#   ti-connectivity— TI WiLink (some x86 tablets/embedded)
#   libertas       — Marvell 88w8xxx (mwifiex)
#   zd1211         — ZyDAS ZD1211 USB adapters
# NOTE: there is NO separate `firmware-mediatek` package in Debian bookworm (it was
# split out only in Debian 13/trixie). The MediaTek MT7921/MT7922 + mt76 blobs ship
# inside firmware-misc-nonfree (already installed above), so MediaTek Wi-Fi is
# covered without it — naming the nonexistent package aborted the ISO build with
# "E: Unable to locate package firmware-mediatek".
firmware-ti-connectivity
firmware-libertas
firmware-zd1211
# git: used by the best-effort out-of-tree Wi-Fi driver hook (Realtek chips Debian
# doesn't package — see hooks/normal/8100-sysible-wifi-drivers.hook.chroot).
git
# 2013-2015 MacBook / Air / Pro Wi-Fi is a Broadcom BCM4360, which the open drivers
# don't drive — it needs the proprietary `wl` module. broadcom-sta-dkms builds wl
# against the shipped kernel headers at image-build time and installs a modprobe
# blacklist for the conflicting open drivers (b43/brcmsmac/bcma/ssb) so wl binds the
# card. This needs linux-headers-amd64 (matching linux-image-amd64), and the wl
# source only compiles on x86 — so both are injected by build.sh into an
# amd64-ONLY list (sysible-hw.list.chroot). They must NOT live in this shared list:
# on an arm64 build linux-headers-amd64 has no candidate (and the wl DKMS build
# would fail), either of which aborts `lb build` — the same arch-split build.sh
# already does for grub-efi-<arch>.
live-boot
live-config
live-config-systemd
calamares
calamares-settings-debian
# Guest integration for VMware/VirtualBox/KVM (clipboard, auto-resize, folder
# sharing) — Sysible Workstation commonly runs in a VM, incl. Fusion on Apple Silicon.
open-vm-tools
open-vm-tools-desktop
# Right-click "Open in SysTerm": Nautilus python extension. The GNOME desktop-
# icons (DING) and dash-to-dock extensions are installed best-effort by the
# system hook (their Debian package names vary), so a missing one never aborts
# the ISO build.
python3-nautilus
firefox-esr
gnome-text-editor
# net-tools ships ifconfig/route/netstat. GNOME does NOT pull it, so name it here
# to guarantee it's present. It installs into /usr/sbin, which is NOT on a regular
# user's PATH by default on Debian — so ifconfig reads as "not installed" in a
# terminal even though it is. /etc/environment (shipped in includes.chroot) puts
# the sbin dirs on PATH for every session (graphical terminal, SSH, tty), and
# /etc/profile.d/sysible-path.sh does the same for login shells.
net-tools
# openssh-server: SSH is ON by default on Sysible Workstation and accepts password
# logins (see the 9000 system hook, which enables ssh and sets PasswordAuthentication
# yes in the stock /etc/ssh/sshd_config — no Sysible drop-in). GNOME core does not
# pull sshd, so it must be named here or `systemctl enable ssh` has nothing to enable.
openssh-server
# Graphical boot/shutdown splash — themed as Sysible (the system hook sets our
# theme as the default; the installer rebuilds the target initramfs so the splash
# lands on installed systems), replacing Debian's Plymouth theme.
plymouth
plymouth-themes
# glib-compile-resources — used by the system hook to rebuild the GNOME Shell
# theme gresource with the Sysible lock/login background.
libglib2.0-dev-bin
# SysTerm runtime deps (it's a GTK3/VTE terminal). The SysTerm .deb is built and
# included by build.sh, but gnome-core doesn't pull the VTE typelib — without
# these its dependency is unmet, so it fails to install/run and "Open Terminal"
# does nothing. Install them explicitly so SysTerm is present and launches.
python3-gi
gir1.2-gtk-3.0
gir1.2-vte-2.91
# Fallback terminal backstop. Every "Open Terminal" on Sysible routes through
# the sysible-term shim to SysTerm; if a bad SysTerm update ever fails to launch,
# the shim falls back to xterm so the user is never locked out of all terminals.
# Tiny, dependency-light, and guaranteed present for exactly this reason.
xterm
